From be9903cb11d48c4119726058d57ce30a6c370eb5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joachim Wiberg <troglobit@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 22:16:43 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 140/156] net: sparx5: weight the queues on the element that
 schedules them
Organization: Wires

On LAN969x the weights of an ets qdisc had no effect, and the lowest
strict band was weighted along with the weighted ones.  With

    tc qdisc add dev eth0 root handle 1: ets bands 8 strict 4 \
        quanta 4500 4500 500 500

two bands of equal weight shared the port strictly, the higher queue
first, and queue 4 shared the port with queues 0 to 3 instead of
taking precedence over them.

The driver wrote the weights to a layer picked per chip, layer 2 on
SparX-5 and layer 1 on LAN969x, at the element with the port's index,
one DWRR entry per queue, as if the queues fed that element at inputs
0 to 7.  The chip builds the scheduler hierarchy itself and the driver
never checked where the queues go.  SE_DWRR_CNT was also written as
the number of weighted queues, but the register counts from one: "0:
No inputs uses DWRR, 1: 2 lowest inputs used DWRR, n: (n+1) lowest
inputs uses DWRR", per the LAN969x register description, and the
vendor API subtracts one as well.

Read HSCH_L0_CFG of the port's queue elements for the layer, element
and input each feeds, and write the weights there by input, with the
count as the highest weighted input, zero for none.  Refuse the
offload when the queues do not share an element, or a strict queue
sits below a weighted one, since the element serves its lower inputs
weighted and the rest strict.

Signed-off-by: Joachim Wiberg <troglobit@gmail.com>
---
